        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "ba06650e-f9d7-3aa4-9b28-a1dec862fa8c",
                "word": "circonlocution",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "b4b75c21-3962-4807-8342-23fd38354f52",
                        "entry_id": "ba06650e-f9d7-3aa4-9b28-a1dec862fa8c",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "circonlocutions"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "03826dd9-223f-3b92-8632-9e1f17b7d6bc",
                        "entry_id": "ba06650e-f9d7-3aa4-9b28-a1dec862fa8c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Circuit de paroles dont on se sert quand on veut évoquer une chose sans l’exprimer directement."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "rhetoric"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4def4711-9637-4912-8a51-ea82e8a0d7ee",
                                "sense_id": "03826dd9-223f-3b92-8632-9e1f17b7d6bc",
                                "text": "Ce mot est expressif, intelligible, et nous en avons besoin. On ne peut y suppléer que par cette circonlocution : j’ai de l’aversion pour la malpropreté. Mais que ce tour est traînant !"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9aafcc92-360b-4192-921d-8dda85ade290",
                                "sense_id": "03826dd9-223f-3b92-8632-9e1f17b7d6bc",
                                "text": "Le lendemain, madame Grandet commença par des protestations infinies. Pendant ces circonlocutions bien longues, M. Leuwen restait grave et impassible."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b905e930-c53a-4fc8-94d1-f497d4f6b9eb",
                                "sense_id": "03826dd9-223f-3b92-8632-9e1f17b7d6bc",
                                "text": "Dans le cours de mes investigations, je rencontrai une chambrette remplie d’une grande quantité d’urnes de faïence d’une forme intime et suspecte. Ces vases peu étrusques me surprirent par leur nombre, et je me dis : « Voilà un chargement des moins poétiques ! Ô Delille, pudique abbé, roi de la périphrase, par quelle circonlocution aurais-tu désigné dans ton alexandrin majestueux cette poterie domestique et nocturne ? »"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "aa85d541-ac5f-44f1-a9f9-6bdaad77de6a",
                                "sense_id": "03826dd9-223f-3b92-8632-9e1f17b7d6bc",
                                "text": "Parlez sans tergiverser, Babillard, nous n’avons pas le temps d’écouter vos circonlocutions, […], la nature indienne l’emporte toujours chez vous, et jamais vous ne pouvez franchement venir au fait."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f6e0ccaf-7984-4b6d-a9e2-bb138674e811",
                                "sense_id": "03826dd9-223f-3b92-8632-9e1f17b7d6bc",
                                "text": "Ici la rédaction se perdait comme dans des sables en d’extraordinaires circonlocutions et retouches de politesse."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3a5fac80-13d4-4e23-9f6b-d87bd1df8661",
                                "sense_id": "03826dd9-223f-3b92-8632-9e1f17b7d6bc",
                                "text": "Les prudentes circonlocutions de l’inspecteur départemental lui revenaient en mémoire : « Vous allez avoir affaire à des gens qui sont comme leurs montagnes : pauvres, pittoresques et d’abord difficile. »"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3d0746a0-3b1b-4b63-bc94-3c805505d631",
                                "sense_id": "03826dd9-223f-3b92-8632-9e1f17b7d6bc",
                                "text": "— C’est aux sultans et aux cadis qu’il faut parler avec des circonlocutions. Pas au Créateur. Dieu est grand, Il n’a que faire de nos petits airs et de nos petites courbettes."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "116d2aa6-c741-4208-afd7-50acb8a6f5db",
                        "source_entry_id": "ba06650e-f9d7-3aa4-9b28-a1dec862fa8c",
                        "source_sense_id": "03826dd9-223f-3b92-8632-9e1f17b7d6bc",
                        "target_lang_word": "circumlocution",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "circumlocution",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2d2a12e3-5d59-3130-8aa4-ef8bad29ba01",
                                        "entry_id": "33e31524-3716-3fb2-ba10-54e8817659a7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A roundabout or indirect way of speaking; thus"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "88517415-c412-45c5-8c86-52101dd06aec",
                                                "sense_id": "2d2a12e3-5d59-3130-8aa4-ef8bad29ba01",
                                                "text": "Circumlocution is a large deſcription either to ſette forth a thyng more gorgiouſlie, or els to hyde it, if the eares cannot beare the open ſpeakyng: or when with fewe woordes we cannot open our meanyng, to ſpeake it more largely."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ced92729-821f-4f4a-a5cd-9fe24598db2b",
                                                "sense_id": "2d2a12e3-5d59-3130-8aa4-ef8bad29ba01",
                                                "text": "His Majeſty leaves the choice to your ſelf, and requires from you a direct Anſwer without circumlocution or bargaining with him […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "feb063e2-d5ff-305b-a04b-18e93ce1ade8",
                                        "entry_id": "33e31524-3716-3fb2-ba10-54e8817659a7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A roundabout or indirect way of speaking; thus:",
                                            "Unnecessary use of extra words to express an idea, such as a pleonastic phrase (sometimes driven by an attempt at emphatic clarity) or a wordy substitution (the latter driven by euphemistic intent, pedagogic intent, or sometimes loquaciousness alone)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "454c1937-9dcf-38d7-a174-7df17a8ba3a1",
                                        "entry_id": "33e31524-3716-3fb2-ba10-54e8817659a7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A roundabout or indirect way of speaking; thus:",
                                            "Necessary use of a phrase to circumvent either a vocabulary fault (of speaker or listener) or a lexical gap, either monolingually or in translation."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c33c5b1c-9a87-4d3e-ad97-f6282236f46f",
                                                "sense_id": "454c1937-9dcf-38d7-a174-7df17a8ba3a1",
                                                "text": "A technical word, such as hyperkalemia or hypoallergenic, can be glossed for general audiences with a circumlocution, such as \"high potassium level\" or \"less likely to cause allergies\" (respectively)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7ec1f4ff-e514-4fbc-9324-ff8c52d978e3",
                                                "sense_id": "454c1937-9dcf-38d7-a174-7df17a8ba3a1",
                                                "text": "It may seem quite straightforward to render line 15 metrically regular by means of a grammatical trick: \"the style as bright as soul\". However, the stylistic feature of the adjectival compound does not occur in isolation but is employed again in the following line, \"dem Staubfaden himmelswüst\", where a grammatical circumlocution in translation would be metrically disadvantageous. Both compounds, \"seelenhell\" as well as \"himmelswüst\", should therefore be translated as compounds. When Michael Hamburger remarks that German \"lends itself to the formation of compound words in a way that English does not\" (in Celan 1988: 24), he probably refers to nouns such as \"Türspalt\" or \"Algenteilchen\"."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ceb7982c-2415-44e3-aeb8-c179bc90be35",
                                                "sense_id": "454c1937-9dcf-38d7-a174-7df17a8ba3a1",
                                                "text": "Linguistic horror vacui again, and lacuna: the blank part of a manuscript where the ink runs off the papyrus, and lexically the word missing from one language that must be bridged with circumlocution in translation."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "44915965-63bf-3c40-b931-02f6e1808415",
                                        "entry_id": "33e31524-3716-3fb2-ba10-54e8817659a7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An instance of such usage; a roundabout expression, whether an inadvisable one or a necessary one."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
